A||||13||jch82||Engine Question???||||5||
Z||000000||jch82||04-26-2001||11:09 PM||||I have the V6 engine and when she gets warmed up (5-10min of running), there is a slight knock at about 4000RPM through every gear......What could this be? Should I be very concerned about it? The engine has almost 140,000mi on it. Any help would be appreciated greatly. Thanks<P>Josh  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||04-26-2001||11:52 PM||||Hey Josh!<BR>What oil you running?<BR>Sounds like rod knock...<BR>crash...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||jch82||04-26-2001||11:57 PM||||Im running 10W30 in her. I was thinking a rod knock but someone told me it might have to do with bearings??? Not really sure what that means (im sorta mechanically inclined)....Any other ideas?<P>Should I be running a thicker grade oil? If so, what grade? Are any of the additives(Marvel Mystery Oil, etc.) worth trying? Thanks Again<P>Josh   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by jch82 (edited 04-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||1988LRC||04-27-2001||02:10 PM||||additives NO, they are only good to mask problems on old high mileage used cars that's why everyone loves used car salesmen<BR>You could try a heavier grade of oil, my personal favorite is Rotella 15-40, if the noise goes away you know that you have worn bearings so an overhaul/rebuild would be in your future||||reg||1
Z||000004||Indiana_resto_guy||04-27-2001||03:05 PM||||That noise means you have worn main and rod bearings. If it were me, I'd get new bearings (checking size first) and a high volume oil pump and put them in now before I wasted my crankshaft.<P>Just my $0.02.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Steven||04-27-2001||04:44 PM||||If you need, I bought a Fiero engine from a guy with the plans of rebuilding it. So that when my engine wore out, I could just swap it in. Well as it turns out, my motor didn't last long enough to do that. And when I started looking at the motor I bought, it looked too good to bore it out or anything. By too good, I mean that the block still had the factory cross hatching on the cylinders, no ridge at the top. The guy said it was a low mileage engine. Looks like he might have been telling the truth. Well I ended up using the heads off of it, because they just plan looked cleaner. If you like I would let the short block go for around $120.00, but we could talk. If you want the heads add $40 to what ever we settle on. That is if your interested.<p>[This message has been edited by Steven (edited 04-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||jch82||04-29-2001||02:36 PM||||So what do new main and rod bearings cost and how much labor is involved? Also the cost of the high volume oil pump? <P>What I would like to do is get this engine to last me all summer without putting too much money into it and then in the fall/winter, I want to put a low mileage 2.8 in along with a clutch and what not. <P>If I ran the higher grade of oil and the noise went away, would the engine last me say the next 4-5months?? Thanks for the help thus far!<P>Josh||||reg||5
Z||000007||jch82||04-29-2001||11:16 PM||||Anyone?<P>||||reg||5
Z||000008||Ken Wittlief||04-29-2001||11:28 PM||||Here is the real truth of mechanical engineering: machines are made in machine shops where they have special equipment that pumps the metal full of noise. the more noise they put into the metal, the more power the resulting machine will have and the longer it will last.<P>  Everytime you run your motor some of that noise leaks out. When all the noise has leaked out, the motor will stop, in fact, when all the noise has leaked out some of the metal will downright disolve and fall apart.<P>   So if something is wrong, and the noise is leaking out faster that it should (ie louder than normal) that means the engine will fail that much sooner. The more the noise, the sooner the failure.<P>   the ultimate noise is subsonic sound, below 20hz, which we can only feel as vibration, or very high frequency noise, a screech or squeal - these represent the worst case noise leakage.<P>   machines that run quiet and smoothly last a long time. Machines that make a lot of noise, squealing, screaching, thumping or vibration are not long for this world.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Indiana_resto_guy||04-30-2001||12:03 AM||||I get mains and rod bearings for around $25.00. High volumn pumps can range anywhere from $50.00 to $85.00, just depends on where I can get them and how fast I need to have it.<BR>If you can't find them for close to that let me know and I'll fix you up with a source.||||reg||1
Z||000010||grinthock||04-30-2001||12:14 AM||||I had the same sound, DO YOURSELF A FAVOUR, fix it NOW, before you damage your crank, rods, or otherwise.<P>DO NOT drive the car anymore.... You risk furthur damage....   I had the same sound, towed the car to the shop, they said I was lucky I did, had I attempted to drive it there I may not have made it, I saved my crank, ended up I spun a bearing, but caught it early enough to save the engine||||reg||1
Z||000011||maryjane||04-30-2001||01:56 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>Here is the real truth of mechanical engineering: machines are made in machine shops where they have special equipment that pumps the metal full of noise. the more noise they put into the metal, the more power the resulting machine will have and the longer it will last.<P>  Everytime you run your motor some of that noise leaks out. When all the noise has leaked out, the motor will stop, in fact, when all the noise has leaked out some of the metal will downright disolve and fall apart.<P>   So if something is wrong, and the noise is leaking out faster that it should (ie louder than normal) that means the engine will fail that much sooner. The more the noise, the sooner the failure.<P>   the ultimate noise is subsonic sound, below 20hz, which we can only feel as vibration, or very high frequency noise, a screech or squeal - these represent the worst case noise leakage.<P>   machines that run quiet and smoothly last a long time. Machines that make a lot of noise, squealing, screaching, thumping or vibration are not long for this world.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Now that is the best explanation I've heard lately of the nature of things mechanical. But how do you figure diesels? They leak noise ALL the time.<BR><P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
